Can you explain how mathematical induction is related to the well-founded properties of natural numbers?
Mathematical induction is a method of mathematical proof typically used to establish a given statement for all natural numbers. It is closely related to the well-foundedness of natural numbers, which means that every non-empty subset of natural numbers has a least element.

The principle of mathematical induction states that if a statement is true for a base case (usually 10) and if the truth of the statement for a given natural number n implies its truth for the next natural number n+1, then the statement is true for all natural numbers. This is equivalent to saying that the set of natural numbers is well-founded, as any non-empty set of natural numbers has a greatest element.

